    "media:imagetext": "Page 4.\npilots killed directly or indireetly due to lack of runneys.\nNow when the\nstable is empty they are working feverishly to complete runways which any and\nevery thinking man with even the remotest knowledge of modern ariation knew\nshould have been provided not later than two or three years ago.\nChengtu on each field 5000 coolies are at work.\npup tents at the field and none can go home until the job is complete. In the\nmeantime for example the Japanese night before last, caught three large planes\n(One being CIA's old Ford) in a small field north of Hanicow, where these planes\nwere stuck in the mud, and bored them to Davy dones locker or wisever a good\nold airplane goes at\nthe finish.\nbecause\nthe lest few days.\nle had\nto\nwait five days to get seats on the Suresis plane and then got the\nlast ones avail-\nable before Dec. 5th, Trains run very in requently to Canton - take forever to get\nthere and are bombed by the Japs all a ong the way.\nSo it was pretty much a case\nof Hobson's choice.\nIt has rained steadily for two weels end the ground is so\nseturated thet the water simply can't sink in and there are no dreins to carry it\nwith much in the way of misgivings,\nWe arrive half under water and the rest slightly above.\nat the airport ond found\nThirteen passengers\nclimbed into the trimotor Junkers including two Buddha-built Germans equivelent\nto\nat least four ordinary people, a crew of four and in the sisle was a large trunk\nbelonging to the only woman passerger who happens to be the number one concubine\nof Sun Fo, President of the legislative Yuen end only son of Sun Yat Sen, therefore\nentitled to carry whetever weight she pleases with the excess baggage charges going\non Sun Fo's well marked\nhe could got oft all right slong the\nHe figured he would\nstop on the take off if he wasn't reasonably sure of making it end even if he did\nstall-in, the ground wes so soft that nobody would get hurt furning at\nTe taxied by some old\nbuildings that formerly belonged to Cheng Hsueh-liang - and\nthe short tield, lets wheel marks b to six inches deep•\nThe pilot \"poured the\ncoal\" and we got off very nicely without too much nerve tension. I'm sure that\nI helped 112t the old Junkers out of the mad.\nWe were cartainly relieved when we\ngot flying\nspeed and started to climb.\nWe flew over and around rain soaked Hankow\nand then climbing all the time went up to 8000 feet and into the clouds.\nthe douds with a solid benk below us - I hope no Jap planes are\nup and around these parts because while they probably wouldn't take any liberties\nit would be embarrassing to have one come around end I have no murads to reach for\n- nothing but Hatemens - Ruth Stevens Weed please note.\nAotually on this flight-\nnow that we've got selely off- I'm not so worried ebout the Jap planes as the\nChinese anti a reraft guns.\nthose runners do not know one\n6d toere ate more tunnes Myse unead the change plane he another end.\nall in fevor of the gun crews.\nLater. Just sighted Bias Bey south of longkong and now we're over British\nterritory.\nEmbarking on this trip I thought there would be lots of interesting\nexperiences to write about.\nNow as the trip is ending I em fraid there wasn't\nmuch to write about after all and that I have written a great deal about very\nlittle.\nAce 139\nBagpio -\nDias Motem -\nTina He us, 5 am St Pance.\nadmira -\nSam ruadin",
